# Single origin (docs/ARCHITECTURE.md §11.1).
#
# `/api/*` -> FastAPI, everything else -> Next.js, both under the same host.
# This keeps the session cookie first-party: no CORS with credentials, no
# SameSite=None, no fighting browser protections that harden every year.
#
# The proxy does TLS termination and nothing else. It never authenticates and
# never injects identity headers — the API would then have to trust them, which
# is invariant I6 displaced by one hop.

# Every address is a variable, because the same file serves a laptop and a host
# (I8). Locally the defaults apply and nothing needs to be set; on a platform
# whose services carry their own DNS names, the three below are.
#
#   PORT      — what the platform tells the container to listen on
#   API_UPSTREAM / WEB_UPSTREAM — where the two others answer
#
# On Railway that is `api.railway.internal:8000` and `web.railway.internal:3000`,
# and both MUST be listening on `::`: the private network is IPv6-only, so a
# service bound to 0.0.0.0 is unreachable here while looking perfectly healthy
# to itself. See the two Dockerfiles.
:{$PORT:8080} {
	handle /api/* {
		reverse_proxy {$API_UPSTREAM:api:8000}
	}

	handle {
		reverse_proxy {$WEB_UPSTREAM:web:3000}
	}
}

# No response timeout is set, and that is deliberate rather than an omission.
# ARCHITECTURE.md §9 keeps generation SYNCHRONOUS on the argument that nothing
# in the path browser -> Caddy -> FastAPI imposes one; a week takes ~30 s on the
# cloud model and was measured at 182 s locally. Adding a timeout here would
# silently invalidate that decision, and the symptom would be a 504 on the
# slowest households — the ones the product exists for.
#
# TLS: on a platform that terminates it at its own edge, this listens on plain
# HTTP behind it and there is nothing to configure. To have Caddy do it
# instead, replace the site address with the domain — a certificate is then
# provisioned automatically:
#
# meal-planner.example.com {
# 	handle /api/* {
# 		reverse_proxy {$API_UPSTREAM:api:8000}
# 	}
# 	handle {
# 		reverse_proxy {$WEB_UPSTREAM:web:3000}
# 	}
# }
